winemac, dwmapi: show what DXMT draws in windows that also paint, and keep their transparency

Two things kept a launcher's Direct3D window from looking as it does on Windows. Both showed
with the GRYPHLINK launcher (Qt 5 drawing through ANGLE on Direct3D 11, in a translucent
window with round corners); neither is particular to it.

A blank window. The Mac driver hides a window's client view (the one Direct3D, OpenGL or
Vulkan draws into) whenever the window is painted the ordinary way, so that the paint is not
covered. Wine's own Direct3D and OpenGL announce each frame they present, which shows the view
again. DXMT presents straight to Metal and announces nothing, so one ordinary paint (Qt fills
its window once as it starts) left the view hidden for good: a white window with frames being
presented into it. A view DXMT presents into is no longer hidden by a paint. And when another
client surface leaves a window (a second swap chain, a surface made only to probe OpenGL or
Vulkan), DXMT's most recent view on that window is shown again.

A black margin. A translucent window drawn with Direct3D relies on Windows' compositor (DWM)
to honour the alpha in its swap chain, and asks for that with DwmEnableBlurBehindWindow. Wine
has no compositor, and the margin around the launcher's rounded window came out black. dwmapi
now marks such a window (a window property, and a SetWindowPos that changes nothing so the
driver notices), and the driver makes the window and its Metal layer non-opaque, so the Mac's
window server composites the alpha instead. DXMT declares every layer opaque, so the layer is
a subclass that knows better. Such a window is not given FineWine's own round corners: it
draws its shape itself. This is the fix patches/x64/stage1-macos 0009 and 0010 made for
CrossOver's Wine, done again for this one.

